<section aria-labelledby="kubernetes-ai-example">
  <h2 id="kubernetes-ai-example">Example: investigate CrashLoopBackOff after a deployment</h2>
  <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-brand-500 pl-5 text-zinc-200">
    Investigate the restarting containers in this namespace. Check pod state, warning
    events, previous-container logs, and rollout history. Explain the failure before proposing a rollback.
  </blockquote>
  <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5">
    <li>
      Select the affected pod and container from the pod listing, then inspect <a href="#action-kubernetes.events_for_pod">events for that pod</a>.
      Read previous-container logs when they are available.
    </li>
    <li>
      Check the workload's rollout status and history. Use the deployment details and
      container errors to decide whether to roll back or try another fix.
    </li>
    <li>
      If a rollback is needed, request it through emisar. Your policy decides whether it
      can run. Check the rollout and application health again when it finishes.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Reading logs is medium risk because they can contain sensitive data. Reading workload
    descriptions or YAML is high risk because environment variables can contain readable
    passwords or other secrets. Review access to these reads as well as changes.
  </p>
</section>
